---
/**
 * SPEC §8: who built it, why, and the disclaimer in full.
 *
 * The footer carries the disclaimer on every page; this is where it appears
 * unabridged, alongside the things a reader needs in order to judge how much
 * weight to put on the figures — who assembled them, what the update cadence
 * is, and what happens when a refresh fails.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import meta from "../data/meta.json";
import screener from "../data/screener.json";

const total = screener.companies.length;
const maxStaleRuns = meta.thresholds.max_stale_runs;
---

<Base title="About">
  <p class="muted">
    <a href="/">Home</a> · <a href="/screener/">Screener</a> ·
    <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>
  </p>

  <h1>About</h1>

  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>
    A screener over {total} US-listed healthcare companies, built from primary sources only: SEC
    EDGAR XBRL company facts for fundamentals, and ClinicalTrials.gov for pipeline. Every figure
    on the site traces to a filing or a registry record, and every company page links to the
    filings its numbers were read from.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing is estimated, smoothed or adjusted. Figures are printed exactly as filed — unrounded
    and unscaled — and where a figure cannot be established honestly it is reported as
    <strong>null</strong>, which is not zero, rather than filled in. The
    <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> gives every formula and its known limitations.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Healthcare screeners generally either cover fundamentals well and ignore the pipeline, or
    cover the pipeline and treat the financials as an afterthought. For a sector where a
    clinical-stage company's value is mostly its trials and its runway, both halves have to be on
    the same page to be worth anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second reason is the one that shaped most of the build: derived metrics are easy to
    compute and hard to compute honestly. Quarterly figures have to be differenced out of
    year-to-date filings, filers migrate XBRL tags mid-life, annual figures get restated after
    their quarters are filed, and a ratio across any of those produces a number that looks
    entirely reasonable and means nothing. This site's main design commitment is refusing to
    print those — and saying so where it has declined.
  </p>

  <h2>Who built it</h2>
  <p class="stale-banner">
    <strong>To fill in before this goes public.</strong> Name, background, and how to get in
    touch. Anyone weighing these figures should be able to see who assembled them.
  </p>

  <h2>How it stays current</h2>
  <p>
    The pipeline re-fetches every company weekly and commits the result, which rebuilds the site.
    Fundamentals move quarterly, so weekly is generous. Current data is from
    <strong>{meta.generated_at}</strong>, pipeline version {meta.pipeline_version}.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a refresh fails for one company, that company keeps its previous figures and is marked
    on both the screener and its own page with the date those figures actually come from — the
    rest of the universe still updates. That fallback is bounded: after {maxStaleRuns} consecutive
    failed refreshes the company is dropped from the site entirely rather than carried further,
    because month-old numbers presented as current are a worse failure than an admitted gap.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not cover</h2>
  <p>
    No prices, market caps or valuation multiples, so nothing here is a valuation. No IFRS
    filers, because IAS 38 capitalises qualifying development costs where US GAAP expenses them,
    which would make R&D intensity and cash runway incomparable across the two. Coverage is
    US-listed and US GAAP only. The <a href="/">home page</a> sets out the exclusions in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Disclaimer</h2>
  <p>
    This site is a personal project for research and educational purposes. It presents publicly
    available data from SEC EDGAR, ClinicalTrials.gov, and openFDA, together with metrics derived
    from that data. Nothing here is investment advice, a recommendation, or an offer to buy or
    sell any security. Figures may be incomplete, delayed, or incorrect.
  </p>
</Base>
